{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"85e1fc324e003cc1","name":"card_event_ijn_akagi","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Japan/NavalWarfare/events/card_event_ijn_akagi","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"IJN AKAGI","text":"Shuffle your hand into your deck, then draw 3 cards. They cost 2 less to play this turn.","flavorText":"The Japanese aircraft carrier Akagi was one of the Imperial Japanese Navy’s most important carriers in the early stages of WWII. Originally laid down as a battlecruiser, she was converted into an aircraft carrier and commissioned in 1927. Akagi became the flagship of the First Air Fleet and played a key role in developing Japan’s carrier strike doctrine, emphasizing coordinated mass air attacks. She was central to Japan’s early successes in the Pacific, in particular the attack on Pearl Harbor and operations in the Philippines, Rabaul, and the Indian Ocean. Akagi carried over 60 aircraft and had a unique three-tiered flight deck early in her career, later rebuilt into a more conventional full-length flight deck. Her powerful air group helped Japan dominate the seas early in the war. However, her success ended at the Battle of Midway in June 1942. While caught off guard by an American carrier aircraft, the Akagi was hit by bombs that caused uncontrollable fires and as a result she was eventually scuttled by Japanese destroyers. Akagi’s loss at Midway marked a turning point in the Pacific War, symbolizing the beginning of Japan’s decline in naval air power.","faction":"Japan","type":"order","rarity":"Rare","cost":4,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"GameplayTags":["subtype.navy"],"Name":"card_event_ijn_akagi","title":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_ijn_akagi_title","SourceString":"IJN AKAGI","LocalizedString":"IJN AKAGI"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Japan","kredits":4,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_ijn_akagi_text","SourceString":"Shuffle your hand into your deck, then draw 3 cards. They cost 2 less to play this turn.","LocalizedString":"Shuffle your hand into your deck, then draw 3 cards. They cost 2 less to play this turn."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_ijn_akagi_history","SourceString":"The Japanese aircraft carrier Akagi was one of the Imperial Japanese Navy’s most important carriers in the early stages of WWII. Originally laid down as a battlecruiser, she was converted into an aircraft carrier and commissioned in 1927. Akagi became the flagship of the First Air Fleet and played a key role in developing Japan’s carrier strike doctrine, emphasizing coordinated mass air attacks. She was central to Japan’s early successes in the Pacific, in particular the attack on Pearl Harbor and operations in the Philippines, Rabaul, and the Indian Ocean. Akagi carried over 60 aircraft and had a unique three-tiered flight deck early in her career, later rebuilt into a more conventional full-length flight deck. Her powerful air group helped Japan dominate the seas early in the war. However, her success ended at the Battle of Midway in June 1942. While caught off guard by an American carrier aircraft, the Akagi was hit by bombs that caused uncontrollable fires and as a result she was eventually scuttled by Japanese destroyers. Akagi’s loss at Midway marked a turning point in the Pacific War, symbolizing the beginning of Japan’s decline in naval air power.","LocalizedString":"The Japanese aircraft carrier Akagi was one of the Imperial Japanese Navy’s most important carriers in the early stages of WWII. Originally laid down as a battlecruiser, she was converted into an aircraft carrier and commissioned in 1927. Akagi became the flagship of the First Air Fleet and played a key role in developing Japan’s carrier strike doctrine, emphasizing coordinated mass air attacks. She was central to Japan’s early successes in the Pacific, in particular the attack on Pearl Harbor and operations in the Philippines, Rabaul, and the Indian Ocean. Akagi carried over 60 aircraft and had a unique three-tiered flight deck early in her career, later rebuilt into a more conventional full-length flight deck. Her powerful air group helped Japan dominate the seas early in the war. However, her success ended at the Battle of Midway in June 1942. While caught off guard by an American carrier aircraft, the Akagi was hit by bombs that caused uncontrollable fires and as a result she was eventually scuttled by Japanese destroyers. Akagi’s loss at Midway marked a turning point in the Pacific War, symbolizing the beginning of Japan’s decline in naval air power."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Rare","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::NavalWarfare","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnEndOfTurn"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Sleeping_Dragon_Drums_2'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Sleeping_Dragon_Drums_2.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Japan/NavalWarfare/t_akagi.t_akagi","SubPathString":""},"artist":"Jarosław Wróbel","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/IJN_AKAGI.IJN_AKAGI","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":467.0,"Y":512.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_ijn_akagi_photo","SourceString":"Japanese aircraft carrier Akagi in April, 1942.","LocalizedString":"Japanese aircraft carrier Akagi in April, 1942."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""},"isRemoval":true},"variables":[{"name":"affectedCards","type":"TArray<int32>","direction":"in"},{"name":"cardIDs","type":"TArray<int32>","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_event_ijn_akagi","owner":"card_event_ijn_akagi_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"pure":false,"event":false,"flags":"FUNC_Final | FUNC_UbergraphFunction |